| Path | Top person | Share |
|---|---|---|
| `lib/` | Troy Goode | 159/238 (66.8%) |
| `test/` | Troy Goode | 517/966 (53.5%) |
| `.github/` | Douglas Wilson | 118/366 (32.2%), next is Íñigo Marquínez 26.0% |
| `.eslintrc.yml` | Douglas Wilson | 7/7 (100%) |
| `.gitignore` | Douglas Wilson | 4/5 (80%) |
| `HISTORY.md` | Douglas Wilson | 50/64 (78.1%) |
| `LICENSE` | Douglas Wilson | 17/22 (77.3%) |
| `package.json` | Douglas Wilson | 20/40 (exactly 50.0%) |
| `README.md` | Troy Goode | 116/277 (41.9%), next is Jon Church 26.0% |

How I counted:
- **Method:** `git blame -w -M -C` on every tracked file, grouped by directory, with each root file counted on its own.
- **Formatting commits left out:** Fiona Formatter's `62628c0` (re-indent plus quote changes) and three older whitespace-only commits (`b42b8b3`, `e5fe9c8`, `8277075`). I found the old ones by checking which commits have an empty diff when whitespace is ignored. Troy's `b2005d9` adds `// checked` comments, so I counted it as real work.
- **Bots:** I dropped dependabot's 25 lines from both the counts and the totals.
- **Same person, two emails:** I merged `troy@laptop.example` into `<email redacted>` (48 lines). Alejandro Caravaca Puchades already uses one email under both names.

Borderline results:
- **`test/`:** Troy's ownership depends on copy detection. With `-C`, lines copied between test files are credited to whoever first wrote them, which gives Troy 53.5%. Without `-C`, Troy drops to 453/966 (46.9%) and Douglas has 400/966, so `test/` would have no owner. I think `-C` is the more accurate count, but remove that line if you want a stricter rule.
- **`package.json`:** Douglas has exactly half, so "at least half" only holds because the 2 bot lines are left out. If you count them, it is 20/42 and he no longer qualifies.

GitHub accepts email addresses in CODEOWNERS only when they are verified on a GitHub account. I don't know these people's GitHub usernames, so I used their commit emails; replace them with `@username` before committing. I have not created the file.

